  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.732,
            0.064,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Prime Time"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.121,
            0.834,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What about 1, which has only one factor? The number 1 is neither a prime nor a composite number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.174,
            0.833,
            0.218
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How many prime numbers are there from 21 to 30? How many composite numbers are there from 21 to 30?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.227,
            0.663,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can we list all the prime numbers from 1 to 100?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.249,
            0.833,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here is an interesting way to find prime numbers. Just follow the steps given below and see what happens."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.303,
            0.765,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 1:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Cross out 1 because it is neither prime nor composite."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.326,
            0.431,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 2:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Circle 2, and then cross out all multiples of 2 after that, i.e., 4, 6, 8, and so on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.394,
            0.431,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " You will find that the next uncrossed number is 3. Circle 3 and then cross out all the multiples of 3 after that, i.e., 6, 9, 12, and so on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.508,
            0.431,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 4:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The next uncrossed number is 5. Circle 5 and then cross out all the multiples of 5 after that, i.e., 10, 15, 20, and so on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.445,
            0.329,
            0.831,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.445,
                0.329,
                0.831,
                0.586
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.621,
            0.832,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 5:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Continue this process till all the numbers in the list are either circled or crossed out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.674,
            0.548,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "All the circled numbers are prime numbers. All the crossed out numbers, other than 1, are composite numbers. This method is called the Sieve of Eratosthenes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.766,
            0.548,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This procedure can be carried on for numbers greater than 100 also. Eratosthenes was a Greek mathematician who lived around 2200 years ago and developed this method of listing primes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.611,
            0.673,
            0.794,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It is definitely not some magic; there should be a reason why it works."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.567,
            0.65,
            0.831,
            0.883
          ],
